  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 244 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTuesday through Sunday:\n\nA cold front progresses eastward through southern Wisconsin\nTuesday morning, bringing northwesterly winds and a return to\nhighs in the 70s. A few showers remain possible in the morning, \nbut chances are low (~10%). \n\nTuesday night, winds shift northerly but remain light, with lows\nin the low 50s. Low pressure will develop in the central to \nsouthern Plains as high pressure builds into the northern Plains, \nbriefly bringing gusty northerly winds and a few showers near the\nWI/IL border along a wave of warm frontogenesis before high \npressure quickly propagates eastward into the Upper Great Lakes on\nWednesday. Light winds and dry conditions expected through\nWednesday night before the high continues to propagate eastward\nand low pressure ejects from the central Plains northeastward.\n\nAs low pressure approaches the Mississippi Valley on Thursday,\nexpect increasing easterly winds and widespread showers with \nisolated embedded thunderstorms along the warm frontal feature. A\nsecondary low pressure system propagating through the northern\nCanadian Prairies looks to phase with the Mississippi Valley \nsystem Thursday night as high pressure across the Northeast U.S.\nblocks each system from propagating eastward. \n\nExact outcome from this phasing remains in question, although \nmodeling has come into agreement that there will be \nintensification during the phasing process, increasing \nnorthwesterly winds by Friday and bringing in a significantly \ncolder airmass as precipitation exits into Friday afternoon. \nExpect highs in the 50s through the weekend with continued \nnorthwesterly winds, and lows in the 30s. With more intense, \ndeeper solutions for the low, lows in the 20s would become \npossible. However, highest confidence at this time is for a more \nmiddle-ground solution of a double-barrelled low rather than a \nvery deep single low, making the 20s potential very low. Will \ncontinue to monitor trends as the event approaches.\n\nMH",
